│ Review background:                                                                                                   │
│ Seizures can present at any time before or after diagnosis of a brain tumor. The risk of seizures varies by tumor    │
│ type and its location in the brain. For a long time we believed that preventing seizures with antiepileptic drugs    │
│ (seizure prophylaxis) was effective and necessary, but the supporting evidence was little and mixed. Such evidence   │
│ was the basis for previous reviews to conclude that seizure prophylaxis was ineffective in people with brain tumors. │
│                                                                                                                      │
│ Review objective:                                                                                                    │
│ To estimate the effectiveness of seizure prophylaxis in people with brain tumors, and to estimate the adverse event  │
│ rates in the identified clinical trials.                                                                             │
│                                                                                                                      │
